  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure displaced just to our north will gradually drift\neastward toward the east coast on Wednesday and Thursday.\nSoutherly flow on the backside of the departing high will bring\nwell above normal temperatures (highs in the 70s, lows in\n40s/50s), potentially challenging record high temperatures on \nThursday (see climate section below).\n\nA low pressure system located well to the north near the Georgian\nBay will bring a cold front across the area Thursday night. \nMoisture will be severely lacking and weak forcing will limit \nrain potential, with only isolated rain showers expected \nThursday night. Rain chances are bit higher (40-50%) in Erie, PA\nwhere Lake Erie may provide a bit more low-level\nmoisture/convergence.\n\nHigh pressure builds back in Friday and Saturday, and ushering\n\"cooler\" but still above normal temperatures with highs in the\n60s and lows in the 40s/50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAs high pressure departs to the east, broad isentropic lift\nbrings rain chances back Sunday and into early next week.\nEnsemble QPF mean calls for around 0.25\" with very low\nprobabilities of an isolated 1\", which means this rain won't be\nvery impactful. However, long-range ensemble guidance is hinting\nat a potential pattern shift with rain chances continuing next\nweek.",
